tive much less  counterproductive i think they are  focusing much more on highlighting what  the science says and making the moral  case that this current generation has to  step up and tackle this challenge rather  than as i say these disruptive protests  that i think alienate a lot of uh kind  of middle ground voters in particular  days if you look at what's happening in  rome today the g20 leaders are there  they're going to come up with some sort  of agreement we hope about climate  action going into glasgow but the youth  movement they just don't have a seat at  the table there  yeah i think the idea of having a seat  at the table is something that we all  need to discuss because young people  don't have a seated table vulnerable  frontline communities don't have a seat  at the table uh which tends to be ethnic  minority people and people from working  class backgrounds um and in this cop  sadly because of covered and vaccine  apartheid we're seeing less people have  their deserved seat at this table um  where are the indigenous groups uh where  are the delegates why aren't we making  decisions not just as governments but as  communities as a civil society  and the interaction between civil  society and cop has been so far so far  um so there is that kind of question of  not just young people why don't we have  a seat at the table  but in entirely why do people who sit on  the margins of society don't have a seat  on the table when we are the first to be  hit by climate change  sam what do you think do you think cop  26 is going to be different this time  around it's going to be more  representative  um i think it will do i mean obviously  it's incredibly difficult and because of  covert  to to get lots of people to glasgow  they're going to be limits on the number  of people able to attend some of the  blue zone events inside the kind of  formal u.n space each day because we  don't want to see a massive spike in  kobe case cases similarly i think there  are you know they're obviously issues as  well with people traveling and having to  undergo quarantine restrictions and uh  and also accessing what is a very busy  hotel and uh an airbnb uh market in  glasgow i think is proving very  challenging but i think the government  has done everything it can do um  realistically within the space of  uh in the  constraint sorry of uh of covid's it's  made vaccines available for delegations  coming from overseas it's made some of  the travel restrictions a bit easier to  manage for some of those delegations so  i think given given the difficult  circumstances it is as uh is open as  possible i think there'll be a strong  civil society voice there at copter to  make sure that world leaders you know  understand and hear some of these  different perspectives as they hopefully  come to an agreement in a couple of  weeks time well let's hope let's leave  the youth movement and representation  and move now to art because as we saw  earlier an artist has rolled his way to  glasgow with the hope of encouraging  people to protect the environment but  can art really inspire change  dave what did you think did you see him  in his metal ball  yes and i absolutely loved it and  weirdly enough that's not the only  really important installation that's  happening at cop and there are many like  for example the air pollution pods which  have taken air pollution from many  different parts of the world um and have  created like an insulation that people  can basically go into feeling you know  what it's like to be in london versus  what's like to be in sweden and feel the  difference within our air quality um so  i think that's particularly important to  bring arts and also as an artist myself  i work at a local nature reserve um as  an artist in residence um and  going through and seeing how people can  connect back to  um the climate and our mother earth um  and this being the sphere for their  action that they take on climate change  is truly important um i also work within  theater and i'm currently working on a  production um with boundless theater  talking about how can we save the planet  when we are broke and we are young um  and there's something that's quite  amazing about this because it connects  um the idea of climate and the issues on  climate right round to the social issues  um that we face within society that's  aimed towards bringing community into  action with understanding where they sit  within the solutions to climate change  art is a very powerful tool and we  should not mistake it as not  sam what do you think do you think art  can raise awareness and  in turn uh impact policy  i think art can raise awareness i think  it often you know through some you know  very  vivid imagery can uh cut through to  audiences but maybe  political messages or a speech from a  politician sometimes can't so  um i think it can really you know bring  to life some of the threats and some of  the challenges we face from  environmental degradation i mean i  recently to give an example went to see  the wildlife photographer of the year  exhibition and there saw some really  haunting imagery of what we're doing in  terms of habitat degradation and  destruction so i think it can be really  powerful for highlighting some of the  challenges  but i do think that you know ultimately  the the challenge of solving these  problems we need to channel that concern  which i think does exist at quite a high  level already among the public for  around issues like climate change and  habitat loss into specific policies  which ultimately will help us deal with  the challenge  and i think they're you know engaging  people with some of the potential  solutions some of the technologies that  we're going to have to adopt and what  that might mean for their lives as well  as incentivizing them financially to  take up some of those technologies and  shift their behavior um i think though  that's a really key part of getting that  positive response right but i think art  can play  an important role right at the start in  getting people engaged and interested  and aware of some of the challenges  just briefly dave do you think that art  is somewhere where um the youth can  actually come and  uh  interact with climate change  definitely and i think that we should  start expanding what we call art because  art isn't just you know the physical  visual painting but it's the writing  it's the poetry it's the songs you know  it's the protest and i always say you  know protests are one big production um  you know there is visually beautiful  things that happen at protests i think  even today with the making of signs and  people expressing their grief their  fears but also their joy and their love  of the planet through different kinds um  i think something that young people are  doing very well okay fantastic well an  exciting week ahead of us days sam thank  you so much for talking to us  thank you  don't forget to listen to this week's  climate cast on this week's episode our  climate political and science  correspondents discuss why a lack of  finance for developing nations and a  reliance on coal could jeopardize  success at cop26 that's it for now  thanks for watching see you next time  [Music]  you
 

